#{[17592186547059 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547059 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547059 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547059 #edb/ref 1071 "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $60$ times. The archer has a $44%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $30$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547059 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d478ba2f-45cc-54a9-9136-5867345b4ca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547059 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=60$ and $p=0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =26.4 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =33.6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(60)(0.44)=26.4\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(60)(0.44)(1-0.44)=14.784.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(26.4,14.784).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>30).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>30)=P(X\\ge 31)≈P(Y\\ge 30.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 30.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{30.5-26.4}{\\sqrt{14.784}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 1.07) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.07) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.07) \\\\  & =1-0.8577 \\\\  & =0.1423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>30)≈0.142.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1431.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼B(100,0.5),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(43<X<53).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0808\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0778\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0951\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0934\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6915\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6950\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6985\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7258\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7291\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7324\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0507dec-a4ee-5084-b5d0-2e0410c8955c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547076 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.5)=50\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.5)(1-0.5)=25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(50,25).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(43<X<53).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)=P(44\\le X\\le 52)≈P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5) & =P(\\frac{43.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{52.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.30\\le Z\\le 0.50) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z<-1.30) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z\\le -1.30) \\\\  & =Φ(0.50)-Φ(-1.30) \\\\  & =0.6915-0.0968 \\\\  & =0.5947\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)≈0.595.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.5947.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547127 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9084a555-7b38-5c9d-b00d-d9bbbbf670d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547127 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186547076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547132 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547132 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9bdf7a37-260b-5c91-9420-b7356d9a3ce1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547132 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547146 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547146 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1f68138-f113-5b99-8a90-b7a879a9472d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547146 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186546976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547155 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d41a0344-b097-5c59-92d9-263fbe0c0f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186546963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547174 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547174 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f669a272-33c5-5b31-b4d7-071551db4a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547174 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186547059 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
